/* SSS1 Core Textbook Upgrade — original research-informed chapters. */
const T=String.fromCharCode(96);
const SSS1_CORE_TEXTBOOK=[
["SSS 1","General Mathematics","Number and Numeration",
T+"<div class='senior-textbook'><h3>Number and Numeration</h3><p>This original chapter develops place value, number sets, fractions, decimals, percentages, approximation and standard form.</p><h3>Learning objectives</h3><p>Classify common numbers; convert between fractions, decimals and percentages; use standard form; round accurately; and solve practical numerical problems.</p><h3>Number sets</h3><table><tr><th>Set</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Examples</th></tr><tr><td>Natural</td><td>Counting numbers; convention varies on inclusion of 0</td><td>0,1,2,3</td></tr><tr><td>Integers</td><td>Whole numbers and their negatives</td><td>−3,−2,−1,0,1</td></tr><tr><td>Rational</td><td>Can be written p/q, q≠0</td><td>3/4,−2,0.125</td></tr><tr><td>Irrational</td><td>Cannot be written exactly as a ratio of integers</td><td>√2,π</td></tr></table><h3>Standard form</h3><p>Standard form writes a number as <strong>a × 10<sup>n</sup></strong>, where 1≤a&lt;10.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> 4,720,000 = <strong>4.72 × 10<sup>6</sup></strong>; 0.00056 = <strong>5.6 × 10<sup>−4</sup></strong>.</div><h3>Fractions, decimals and percentages</h3><p>Multiply a fraction by 100% to obtain a percentage; divide a percentage by 100 to obtain a decimal. Simplify fractions where appropriate.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> 3/8=0.375=37.5%. A ₦12,000 item discounted by 15% has discount ₦1,800 and sale price <strong>₦10,200</strong>.</div><h3>Approximation</h3><p>Decimal places count digits after the decimal point. Significant figures begin at the first non-zero digit. Do not present a rounded value as exact.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> 0.004936 to 2 significant figures is <strong>0.0049</strong>; to 3 significant figures it is <strong>0.00494</strong>.</div><h3>Practical activity</h3><p>Record five prices, express each as a percentage of ₦10,000, round to two decimal places and compare rounded and unrounded results. Discuss why rounding can matter in budgeting.</p><h3>Common misconceptions</h3><ul><li>Decimal places and significant figures are not the same.</li><li>Rounding intermediate values can introduce extra error.</li><li>Adding fractions requires a suitable common denominator.</li></ul><h3>Exercises with answers</h3><ol><li>0.625 as a fraction: <strong>5/8</strong>.</li><li>0.00072 in standard form: <strong>7.2 × 10<sup>−4</sup></strong>.</li><li>18% of ₦25,000: <strong>₦4,500</strong>.</li><li>7.8462 to 3 significant figures: <strong>7.85</strong>.</li></ol><h3>Exam-style question</h3><p>A trader buys an article for ₦8,000 and makes 12.5% profit. <strong>Solution:</strong> profit=0.125×8,000=₦1,000; selling price=<strong>₦9,000</strong>.</p><h3>Further study</h3><p>Use the current NERDC curriculum and an approved senior-secondary Mathematics text for graded exercises.</p></div>"+T,
"<p><strong>Extra practice:</strong> Convert 5/16 to a decimal and percentage. Express 3,450,000 in standard form. Round 0.006784 to 2 significant figures. A ₦40,000 item is reduced by 7.5%; find the reduction and new price.</p>"],

["SSS 1","English Language","Reading Comprehension and Critical Reading",
T+"<div class='senior-textbook'><h3>Reading Comprehension and Critical Reading</h3><p>This original chapter develops main-idea identification, inference, vocabulary in context, tone, purpose, audience and evidence-based reading.</p><h3>Learning objectives</h3><p>Identify central ideas, distinguish explicit information from inference, interpret words in context, recognise tone and purpose, and support answers with textual evidence.</p><h3>Reading for meaning</h3><p>Do not merely copy words. Identify the subject and purpose, then track cause and effect, comparison, sequence, problem and solution, or claim and evidence.</p><h3>Main idea and supporting details</h3><p>The main idea is the central point developed by the writer. Supporting details may provide evidence, examples, explanations or illustrations. An interesting detail is not necessarily the main idea.</p><h3>Inference</h3><p>An inference is a conclusion supported by clues in the passage. Keep it within the evidence; do not invent facts.</p><div class='box'><strong>Mini-passage:</strong> “The school introduced a reading period before the first lesson. After three months, the library recorded more student visits and teachers reported that more learners were borrowing books.” <strong>Careful inference:</strong> the reading period was associated with increased library use; the passage alone does not prove it was the only cause.</div><h3>Vocabulary in context</h3><p>Use nearby clues, contrast, examples, word parts and the sentence's overall meaning. Do not select a dictionary definition that fails in context.</p><h3>Tone, purpose and audience</h3><p><strong>Tone</strong> is the writer's attitude conveyed by language. <strong>Purpose</strong> is the intended objective. <strong>Audience</strong> is the intended readership. A passage may be informative, persuasive, critical, reflective, humorous or instructional.</p><h3>Critical reading</h3><p>Ask: What claim is made? What evidence supports it? Is the evidence relevant and sufficient? Are assumptions hidden? Is fact distinguished from opinion? Critical reading means evaluating claims carefully.</p><h3>Answering questions</h3><ol><li>Read the question first.</li><li>Locate relevant evidence.</li><li>Answer exactly what was asked.</li><li>Use your own words when explanation or summary is required.</li><li>Keep the response proportional to the marks.</li></ol><h3>Common misconceptions</h3><ul><li>A longer answer is not automatically better.</li><li>An inference must remain anchored to the text.</li><li>Context can often establish meaning without a dictionary.</li></ul><h3>Exercises with answers</h3><ol><li>Define inference. <strong>Answer:</strong> a conclusion drawn from textual evidence and reasoning.</li><li>Differentiate tone and purpose. <strong>Answer:</strong> tone is attitude conveyed by language; purpose is the communication objective.</li><li>Why distinguish association from causation? <strong>Answer:</strong> events occurring together do not automatically prove that one caused the other.</li></ol><h3>Exam-style task</h3><p>Write 120–150 words explaining how a school could increase voluntary reading. Include a clear claim, two developed reasons and one practical example.</p><h3>Further study</h3><p>Use the NERDC English curriculum and approved senior-secondary English texts for progressively harder passages.</p></div>"+T,
"<p><strong>Extra practice:</strong> Choose a school notice or news passage. Identify its purpose, audience, central claim, supporting evidence and tone. Rewrite its main idea in one sentence without adding unsupported information.</p>"],

["SSS 1","Government","Meaning, Scope and Functions of Government",
T+"<div class='senior-textbook'><h3>Meaning, Scope and Functions of Government</h3><p>This original civic/governmental studies synthesis uses the curriculum as its framework. Contemporary constitutional and institutional claims should be checked against current authoritative Nigerian sources because legal arrangements can change.</p><h3>Learning objectives</h3><p>Define government; distinguish government, state and society; identify major functions; explain authority; and analyse how government functions affect citizens.</p><h3>Meaning of government</h3><p>Government refers to the institutions and processes through which authoritative decisions are made and implemented in a political community. It can also refer to the people and institutions temporarily exercising public authority under a constitutional order.</p><h3>Government, state and society</h3><table><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>State</td><td>A political community with territory, population, government and sovereignty.</td></tr><tr><td>Government</td><td>The institutions and office-holders through which public authority is exercised.</td></tr><tr><td>Society</td><td>The wider network of people, relationships, institutions and groups.</td></tr></table><h3>Major functions</h3><ul><li>Maintaining public order and security through lawful institutions.</li><li>Making and enforcing laws within constitutional limits.</li><li>Providing or organising public goods and services.</li><li>Protecting rights and providing dispute-resolution mechanisms.</li><li>Raising and managing public revenue.</li><li>Regulating activities where collective rules are required.</li><li>Conducting external relations and representing the state internationally.</li></ul><h3>Authority, law and accountability</h3><p>Public rules work best when institutions have legitimate authority and mechanisms for review and accountability. Constitutional government constrains public power through law and institutional checks.</p><h3>Government and citizens</h3><p>Citizens interact with government through public services, laws, taxation, elections and other lawful channels. Civic participation includes staying informed, obeying lawful rules, using public institutions responsibly and expressing views peacefully and lawfully.</p><h3>Neutral analysis activity</h3><p>Choose a public service such as a school, road, health facility or waste-management programme. Identify the relevant level of government, the public need, resources involved, accountability mechanisms and measurable performance indicators. Separate documented evidence from opinion.</p><h3>Common misconceptions</h3><ul><li>Government is not identical to the state.</li><li>Government functions are not unlimited.</li><li>Citizenship does not require support for a particular party or office-holder.</li></ul><h3>Exam-style questions and answers</h3><ol><li>Define government. <strong>Answer:</strong> institutions and processes through which authoritative public decisions are made and implemented.</li><li>State four functions. <strong>Answer:</strong> any four correctly explained, such as maintaining order, law-making/enforcement, service provision and revenue management.</li><li>Distinguish government from state. <strong>Answer:</strong> the state is the enduring political community; government is the machinery and office-holders through which public authority is exercised.</li></ol><h3>Further study</h3><p>Use the current NERDC Government curriculum and current Nigerian constitutional/legal sources for specific institutions, powers and procedures.</p></div>"+T,
"<p><strong>Exam practice:</strong> Write a structured answer explaining five functions of government. For each, give a concrete type of public activity and explain why accountability matters.</p>"],
["SSS 1","Digital Technologies","Digital Devices, Operating Systems and File Management",
T+"<div class='senior-textbook'><h3>Digital Devices, Operating Systems and File Management</h3><p>This chapter builds practical digital literacy: identifying devices, understanding operating systems, managing files and folders, and using storage safely.</p><h3>Learning objectives</h3><p>Identify major device categories, distinguish hardware from software, explain the role of an operating system, organise files systematically and apply basic data-safety practices.</p><h3>Hardware and software</h3><table><tr><th>Category</th><th>Examples</th><th>Role</th></tr><tr><td>Input</td><td>Keyboard, mouse, microphone</td><td>Supplies data or commands</td></tr><tr><td>Output</td><td>Monitor, printer, speakers</td><td>Presents results</td></tr><tr><td>Processing</td><td>CPU</td><td>Executes instructions</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>SSD, hard drive, memory card</td><td>Stores data</td></tr><tr><td>Network</td><td>Router, access point</td><td>Connects devices and networks</td></tr></table><h3>Operating systems</h3><p>An operating system manages hardware resources and provides services through which applications run. Examples include Android, Windows, Linux and macOS. It handles tasks such as process management, storage organisation, device interaction and user accounts.</p><h3>Files, folders and paths</h3><p>A file is a named collection of digital data. A folder provides a logical location for organising files. Use descriptive names, consistent folder structures and meaningful extensions. Avoid storing the only copy of important work on one device.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked scenario:</strong> Create a school-project folder, then subfolders for <em>Research</em>, <em>Drafts</em>, <em>Images</em> and <em>Final</em>. Use names such as <strong>biology-cell-notes-v1</strong> rather than <strong>newfile</strong>. Keep dated backup copies when the work is important.</div><h3>Storage and backup</h3><p>Internal storage, removable storage and cloud storage have different risks and access conditions. A backup is a separate copy that can be restored if the original is lost, corrupted or damaged. Backups should be tested periodically.</p><h3>Safety and privacy</h3><ul><li>Use strong, unique passwords and available multi-factor authentication.</li><li>Install software from trusted sources and keep supported software updated.</li><li>Do not open unexpected attachments or give passwords to people who request them casually.</li><li>Protect other people's personal information when creating or sharing files.</li></ul><h3>Practical activity</h3><p>Create a three-level folder structure for a term's schoolwork. Save one document, rename it using a consistent convention, copy it to a backup location and explain how you would restore it after accidental deletion.</p><h3>Common misconceptions</h3><ul><li>RAM is not the same as permanent storage.</li><li>An operating system is not the same thing as every application installed on a device.</li><li>Renaming a file does not automatically create a backup.</li></ul><h3>Exercises with answers</h3><ol><li>State two input devices. <strong>Answer:</strong> keyboard and microphone.</li><li>What is an operating system? <strong>Answer:</strong> system software that manages hardware/resources and provides services for applications and users.</li><li>Why make backups? <strong>Answer:</strong> to provide recoverable copies when the original is lost, corrupted or damaged.</li></ol><h3>Further study</h3><p>Use the current NERDC Digital Technologies curriculum and current manufacturer or security guidance when technical procedures depend on a particular device.</p></div>"+T,
"<p><strong>Extra practice:</strong> Design a file naming convention for a class project containing notes, images, spreadsheets and final submissions. Explain how your convention reduces accidental overwriting.</p>"],,

["SSS 1","Citizenship and Heritage Studies","Identity, Citizenship and National Values",
T+"<div class='senior-textbook'><h3>Identity, Citizenship and National Values</h3><p>This chapter treats citizenship as a civic-learning subject. It distinguishes factual civic concepts from opinions about contemporary political actors or parties.</p><h3>Learning objectives</h3><p>Explain identity and citizenship, distinguish rights from responsibilities, describe national values and analyse how individual conduct contributes to peaceful community life.</p><h3>Identity</h3><p>Identity is the way individuals and communities understand and express who they are. It may include family, language, locality, culture, interests and civic membership. A person can have several identities at the same time.</p><h3>Citizenship</h3><p>Citizenship is a legal and civic relationship between a person and a state. Its exact acquisition, rights and obligations are governed by applicable law. Do not assume that every resident has identical legal status or rights.</p><h3>Values</h3><table><tr><th>Value</th><th>Civic meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Integrity</td><td>Acting honestly and consistently with ethical standards</td></tr><tr><td>Respect</td><td>Recognising the dignity and legitimate rights of others</td></tr><tr><td>Responsibility</td><td>Accepting duties and consequences of one's actions</td></tr><tr><td>Justice</td><td>Applying fair and lawful standards</td></tr><tr><td>Patriotism</td><td>Commitment to the welfare of one's country without requiring hostility to others</td></tr></table><h3>Rights and responsibilities</h3><p>Rights protect legitimate interests and freedoms; responsibilities are duties owed to the community and other people. Civic responsibility can include obeying lawful rules, respecting others, protecting public property, participating constructively in community life and seeking accurate information before sharing claims.</p><h3>National symbols and heritage</h3><p>National symbols communicate shared identity. Heritage includes cultural practices, historical places, languages, arts, literature and other inherited or preserved expressions. Heritage should be studied critically: preservation does not require ignoring historical disagreements or harmful practices.</p><h3>Practical civic activity</h3><p>Choose one community problem such as littering, unsafe roads or damage to public property. Identify the affected people, evidence of the problem, relevant responsibilities, possible lawful actions and a measurable indicator of improvement.</p><h3>Common misconceptions</h3><ul><li>Patriotism does not mean unquestioning approval of every government decision.</li><li>Rights are not a licence to violate other people's lawful rights.</li><li>National identity can coexist with local, cultural and religious identities.</li></ul><h3>Exam-style questions and answers</h3><ol><li>Define citizenship. <strong>Answer:</strong> a legal and civic relationship between a person and a state, governed by applicable law.</li><li>Differentiate rights and responsibilities. <strong>Answer:</strong> rights are protected claims or freedoms; responsibilities are duties expected of members of a community.</li><li>Explain two national values. <strong>Answer:</strong> any two accurately explained, such as integrity and responsibility.</li></ol><h3>Further study</h3><p>For current constitutional or citizenship-law questions, consult authoritative Nigerian legal and government sources rather than relying on an undated summary.</p></div>"+T,
"<p><strong>Extra practice:</strong> Write a one-page community action plan for improving one local problem. Include the evidence you would collect, the people or institutions involved, lawful actions and a way to measure progress.</p>"],,

["SSS 1","Commerce","Meaning, Scope and Importance of Commerce",
T+"<div class='senior-textbook'><h3>Meaning, Scope and Importance of Commerce</h3><h3>Learning objectives</h3><p>Define commerce, distinguish trade from aids to trade, classify home and foreign trade, identify major commercial activities and explain their importance to producers and consumers.</p><h3>Meaning of commerce</h3><p>Commerce covers activities that facilitate the exchange and distribution of goods and services from producers to consumers. It includes <strong>trade</strong> and the <strong>aids to trade</strong> that make trade possible or easier.</p><h3>Trade and aids to trade</h3><table><tr><th>Area</th><th>Examples</th><th>Purpose</th></tr><tr><td>Trade</td><td>Buying and selling</td><td>Transfers goods/services between parties</td></tr><tr><td>Transport</td><td>Road, rail, sea, air</td><td>Moves goods and people</td></tr><tr><td>Warehousing</td><td>Storage facilities</td><td>Preserves goods and balances supply over time</td></tr><tr><td>Banking</td><td>Payments, deposits, credit services</td><td>Facilitates financial transactions</td></tr><tr><td>Insurance</td><td>Risk cover</td><td>Provides financial protection against specified risks</td></tr><tr><td>Communication</td><td>Telephone, internet, postal services</td><td>Transfers information</td></tr><tr><td>Advertising</td><td>Commercial communication</td><td>Informs or persuades potential customers</td></tr></table><h3>Home and foreign trade</h3><p><strong>Home trade</strong> occurs within a country's domestic market. <strong>Foreign trade</strong> involves transactions across national borders and includes imports and exports. Foreign trade requires attention to documentation, transport, exchange arrangements, applicable regulations and payment risk.</p><h3>Why commerce matters</h3><ul><li>Connects producers with consumers.</li><li>Creates employment and enterprise opportunities.</li><li>Supports specialisation and distribution.</li><li>Provides channels for information and market signals.</li><li>Facilitates movement and storage of goods.</li></ul><h3>Worked business scenario</h3><p>A small food producer makes 500 packaged products but customers are spread across several towns. A distributor, transport provider, warehouse, payment service and communication channel can each perform a commercial function. The producer should compare their costs, reliability, risks and customer-service effects rather than choosing solely on price.</p><h3>Practical activity</h3><p>Choose a common product sold locally. Trace its journey from producer to final consumer and identify at least five commercial activities involved. Draw the chain and label where transport, finance, storage, communication and risk management occur.</p><h3>Common misconceptions</h3><ul><li>Commerce is broader than buying and selling alone.</li><li>Transport and warehousing support trade but are not themselves trade in the narrow sense.</li><li>Foreign trade is not simply the same as buying goods in a local market.</li></ul><h3>Exercises with answers</h3><ol><li>Define commerce. <strong>Answer:</strong> activities that facilitate exchange and distribution of goods and services, including trade and aids to trade.</li><li>State four aids to trade. <strong>Answer:</strong> any four such as transport, banking, insurance, warehousing and communication.</li><li>Distinguish home and foreign trade. <strong>Answer:</strong> home trade occurs within the domestic market; foreign trade crosses national borders.</li></ol><h3>Further study</h3><p>Use the current NERDC Commerce curriculum and an approved senior-secondary Commerce text for additional cases and exercises.</p></div>"+T,
"<p><strong>Extra practice:</strong> Select one Nigerian consumer product and map its commercial journey from producer to consumer. Identify the trade activity and at least five aids to trade used along the way.</p>"],

["SSS 1","Biology","Classification of Living Organisms",T+"<div class='senior-textbook'><h3>Classification of Living Organisms</h3><p>Classification organises biological diversity using shared structural, functional and evolutionary characteristics.</p><h3>Taxonomic ranks</h3><table><tr><th>Rank</th><th>Human example</th></tr><tr><td>Kingdom</td><td>Animalia</td></tr><tr><td>Phylum</td><td>Chordata</td></tr><tr><td>Class</td><td>Mammalia</td></tr><tr><td>Order</td><td>Primates</td></tr><tr><td>Family</td><td>Hominidae</td></tr><tr><td>Genus</td><td>Homo</td></tr><tr><td>Species</td><td>Homo sapiens</td></tr></table><h3>Scientific naming</h3><p>Binomial nomenclature uses a genus and specific epithet. The genus begins with a capital letter and the specific epithet normally begins with a lower-case letter.</p><div class='box'><strong>Example:</strong> In <em>Homo sapiens</em>, <em>Homo</em> is the genus and <em>sapiens</em> is the specific epithet.</div><h3>Practical activity</h3><p>Photograph five harmless organisms around the school and construct a simple paired-choice identification key using observable features.</p><h3>Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>Classification is more than naming.</li><li>Superficial resemblance does not necessarily indicate close relationship.</li></ul><h3>Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>Give two reasons for classification. <strong>Answer:</strong> easier identification and organised study/communication.</li><li>Define binomial nomenclature. <strong>Answer:</strong> a system using genus and specific epithet.</li></ol><h3>Exam-style question</h3><p>Explain four characteristics that can be used in classification and give one example for each.</p><h3>Further study</h3><p>Use the current NERDC Biology curriculum and approved Biology texts for extended classification keys.</p></div>"+T,"<p><strong>Further practice:</strong> Complete additional graded problems and explain each solution step.</p>"],
["SSS 1","Chemistry","Chemical Bonding",T+"<div class='senior-textbook'><h3>Chemical Bonding</h3><p>Atoms form chemical bonds when the resulting arrangement is energetically more stable. School-level models include ionic, covalent and metallic bonding.</p><h3>Ionic bonding</h3><p>Ionic bonding is electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions. Metals commonly form positive ions and non-metals commonly form negative ions.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> Na has 2,8,1 and forms Na<sup>+</sup>; Cl has 2,8,7 and forms Cl<sup>−</sup>. Charges balance 1:1, giving <strong>NaCl</strong>.</div><h3>Covalent bonding</h3><p>Covalent bonding involves sharing electron pairs between atoms, commonly between non-metals.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> In the usual electron-pair model, H<sub>2</sub> contains one shared pair and H<sub>2</sub>O contains two O–H bonds.</div><h3>Metallic bonding</h3><p>Metallic structures can be modelled as positive ions with delocalised electrons. This helps explain conductivity, malleability and ductility.</p><h3>Properties</h3><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Typical property pattern</th></tr><tr><td>Ionic</td><td>Often high melting point; conducts when ions are mobile</td></tr><tr><td>Simple molecular covalent</td><td>Often lower melting/boiling points; generally poor conductors</td></tr><tr><td>Metallic</td><td>Conductive, malleable and ductile</td></tr></table><h3>Practical activity</h3><p>Use safe paper or ball-and-stick models to represent an ionic lattice and a covalent molecule. State the limitations of each model.</p><h3>Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>Define ionic bonding. <strong>Answer:</strong> electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions.</li><li>Why is NaCl 1:1? <strong>Answer:</strong> Na<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>−</sup> charges balance in a 1:1 ratio.</li></ol><h3>Exam-style question</h3><p>Explain how Mg<sup>2+</sup> and Cl<sup>−</sup> produce MgCl<sub>2</sub>.</p><h3>Further study</h3><p>Use the NERDC Chemistry curriculum and approved Chemistry texts for Lewis structures and extended bonding.</p></div>"+T,"<p><strong>Further practice:</strong> Complete additional graded problems and explain each solution step.</p>"],
["SSS 1","Physics","Motion in One Dimension",T+"<div class='senior-textbook'><h3>Motion in One Dimension</h3><p>Distance is scalar path length; displacement is directed change in position. Average speed is distance divided by time, while average velocity is displacement divided by time.</p><h3>Acceleration</h3><p>Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity: <strong>a=(v−u)/t</strong>.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> A cyclist changes from 4 m/s to 16 m/s in 6 s. a=(16−4)/6=<strong>2 m/s²</strong>.</div><h3>Constant acceleration</h3><p>Useful equations are <strong>v=u+at</strong>, <strong>s=ut+½at²</strong> and <strong>v²=u²+2as</strong>.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> From rest, a car accelerates at 3 m/s² for 5 s. v=<strong>15 m/s</strong>; s=<strong>37.5 m</strong>.</div><h3>Graphs</h3><p>Displacement-time gradient gives velocity; velocity-time gradient gives acceleration; area under a velocity-time graph gives displacement.</p><h3>Practical activity</h3><p>Measure a trolley's travel time over a known distance in repeated trials. Calculate average speed and discuss timing error.</p><h3>Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>Acceleration means changing velocity, not only speeding up.</li><li>Negative velocity can indicate the chosen negative direction.</li></ul><h3>Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>100 m in 12.5 s: <strong>8.0 m/s</strong>.</li><li>10 to 22 m/s in 4 s: <strong>3.0 m/s²</strong>.</li><li>From rest at 2 m/s² for 6 s: <strong>36 m</strong>.</li></ol><h3>Exam-style question</h3><p>A vehicle at 20 m/s stops uniformly in 5 s. Find acceleration and stopping distance, showing your sign convention.</p><h3>Further study</h3><p>Use the NERDC Physics curriculum and approved Physics texts for mechanics and graph problems.</p></div>"+T,"<p><strong>Further practice:</strong> Complete additional graded problems and explain each solution step.</p>"],
["SSS 1","Economics","Demand and Supply",T+"<div class='senior-textbook'><h3>Demand and Supply</h3><p>Demand is quantity consumers are willing and able to buy at a stated price and time. Supply is quantity producers are willing and able to offer.</p><h3>Movement and shift</h3><p>A change in the good's own price normally causes movement along a curve. Changes in other determinants can shift the curve. Demand determinants include income, tastes, related-goods prices and number of buyers. Supply determinants include input costs, technology, taxes and expectations.</p><h3>Equilibrium calculation</h3><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> Qd=40−2P and Qs=10+P. At equilibrium 40−2P=10+P, so P=<strong>10</strong> and Q=<strong>20</strong>.</div><h3>Practical activity</h3><p>Create a five-price demand and supply schedule, plot both and identify their intersection.</p><h3>Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>Demand is not merely desire; ability and willingness to buy matter.</li><li>A price change is normally a movement, not a curve shift.</li></ul><h3>Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>Qd=50−P and Qs=20+2P: <strong>P=10, Q=40</strong>.</li><li>Two supply determinants: <strong>input costs and technology</strong>.</li></ol><h3>Exam-style question</h3><p>Using Qd=60−2P and Qs=12+P, calculate equilibrium price and quantity. Explain the likely effect of higher production costs.</p><h3>Further study</h3><p>Use the NERDC Economics curriculum and approved Economics texts for elasticity and market structures.</p></div>"+T,"<p><strong>Further practice:</strong> Complete additional graded problems and explain each solution step.</p>"],
["SSS 1","Accounting","Double-Entry Bookkeeping and Source Documents",T+"<div class='senior-textbook'><h3>Double-Entry Bookkeeping and Source Documents</h3><p>Every transaction has at least two accounting effects. Total debits must equal total credits.</p><table><tr><th>Account</th><th>Increase</th><th>Decrease</th></tr><tr><td>Asset</td><td>Debit</td><td>Credit</td></tr><tr><td>Liability</td><td>Credit</td><td>Debit</td></tr><tr><td>Capital</td><td>Credit</td><td>Debit</td></tr><tr><td>Expense</td><td>Debit</td><td>Credit</td></tr><tr><td>Income</td><td>Credit</td><td>Debit</td></tr></table><h3>Source documents</h3><p>Invoices, receipts, payment vouchers, bank records and credit notes can provide evidence for recording transactions.</p><h3>Worked entries</h3><p>Owner introduces ₦300,000 cash: <strong>Dr Cash ₦300,000; Cr Capital ₦300,000</strong>.</p><p>Furniture bought for ₦80,000 cash: <strong>Dr Furniture ₦80,000; Cr Cash ₦80,000</strong>.</p><p>Rent paid ₦20,000: <strong>Dr Rent ₦20,000; Cr Cash ₦20,000</strong>.</p><p>Assets become ₦280,000; capital less rent expense also becomes ₦280,000.</p><h3>Practical activity</h3><p>Create five imaginary school-enterprise transactions. Identify source document, accounts, debit, credit and amount. Check equal debit and credit totals.</p><h3>Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>Debit does not always mean increase; account type matters.</li><li>A source document is evidence, not the ledger.</li></ul><h3>Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>Owner introduces ₦100,000: <strong>Dr Cash; Cr Capital</strong>.</li><li>Pay electricity ₦15,000: <strong>Dr Electricity; Cr Cash</strong>.</li><li>Buy goods on credit ₦50,000: <strong>Dr Purchases; Cr Trade Payable</strong>.</li></ol><h3>Exam-style question</h3><p>Record ₦500,000 capital, ₦120,000 equipment for cash, ₦90,000 credit purchases and ₦30,000 rent. Verify the equation.</p><h3>Further study</h3><p>Use the NERDC Accounting curriculum and approved Accounting texts for journals, ledgers and financial statements.</p></div>"+T,"<p><strong>Further practice:</strong> Complete additional graded problems and explain each solution step.</p>"],
["SSS 1","Further Mathematics","Sets, Functions and Mappings",T+"<div class='senior-textbook'><h3>Sets, Functions and Mappings</h3><p>A set is a well-defined collection. Use ∪ for union and ∩ for intersection. For finite sets, <strong>n(A∪B)=n(A)+n(B)−n(A∩B)</strong>.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> n(A)=25, n(B)=18, n(A∩B)=7. Therefore n(A∪B)=<strong>36</strong>.</div><h3>Functions</h3><p>A function is a relation in which every input in the domain has exactly one output. Different inputs can share an output.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> f(x)=2x+3. Then f(4)=<strong>11</strong>. If f(x)=15, then 2x+3=15 and x=<strong>6</strong>.</div><h3>Practical activity</h3><p>Use two non-sensitive class preferences to form sets. Calculate their union and intersection and explain the results.</p><h3>Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>A function can map different inputs to one output.</li><li>The range is not automatically the whole codomain.</li><li>Union does not count shared members twice.</li></ul><h3>Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>A={2,4,6}, B={4,6,8}: A∩B=<strong>{4,6}</strong>.</li><li>n(A)=30, n(B)=22, n(A∩B)=10: n(A∪B)=<strong>42</strong>.</li><li>f(x)=3x−2: f(5)=<strong>13</strong>.</li></ol><h3>Exam-style question</h3><p>Let A={1,2,3,4,5}, B={2,4,6,8}, U={1,...,8}. Find A∩B, A∪B and the complement of A in U.</p><h3>Further study</h3><p>Use the NERDC Further Mathematics curriculum and approved texts for probability, matrices and advanced functions.</p></div>"+T,"<p><strong>Further practice:</strong> Complete additional graded problems and explain each solution step.</p>"],

["SSS 1","General Mathematics","Algebraic Expressions, Indices and Surds",
T+"<div class='senior-textbook'><h3>Algebraic Expressions, Indices and Surds</h3><p>This chapter develops algebraic language, simplification, expansion, factorisation, laws of indices and basic surd manipulation.</p><h3>Learning objectives</h3><p>Simplify expressions, collect like terms, expand brackets, factorise common forms, apply index laws and simplify simple surds.</p><h3>Algebraic language</h3><p>A term is separated from another term by + or −. A coefficient multiplies a variable, while a constant has no variable factor. Like terms have the same variable parts raised to the same powers.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> 3x+5−2x+7=(3x−2x)+(5+7)=<strong>x+12</strong>.</div><h3>Expansion and factorisation</h3><p>Use the distributive law a(b+c)=ab+ac. Factorisation reverses expansion by identifying a common factor or a suitable algebraic pattern.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> 3x(2x−5)=<strong>6x²−15x</strong>. Conversely, 6x²−15x=<strong>3x(2x−5)</strong>.</div><p>For a difference of two squares, a²−b²=(a−b)(a+b).</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> x²−25=(x−5)(x+5).</div><h3>Indices</h3><p>For non-zero a, a^m×a^n=a^(m+n), a^m÷a^n=a^(m−n), and (a^m)^n=a^(mn). Also a^0=1. Fractional indices connect powers with roots, for example a^(1/2)=√a for a≥0.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> 2³×2⁴=2⁷=128. Also 27^(2/3)=(∛27)²=3²=<strong>9</strong>.</div><h3>Surds</h3><p>A surd is an irrational root expressed exactly, such as √2. Simplify by taking out perfect-square factors: √72=√(36×2)=<strong>6√2</strong>.</p><h3>Practical activity</h3><p>Measure the dimensions of a rectangular classroom area, represent its length and width algebraically, expand the area expression and discuss how changing one dimension changes the area.</p><h3>Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>2x+3x=5x, but 2x+3y cannot be combined into 5xy.</li><li>a^m+a^n is generally not a^(m+n).</li><li>√(a+b) is generally not √a+√b.</li></ul><h3>Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>Simplify 7x−3+2x+5. <strong>Answer: 9x+2.</strong></li><li>Expand 4(2x−3). <strong>Answer: 8x−12.</strong></li><li>Factorise 5x²+10x. <strong>Answer: 5x(x+2).</strong></li><li>Simplify √50. <strong>Answer: 5√2.</strong></li><li>Simplify 3²×3³. <strong>Answer: 3⁵=243.</strong></li></ol><h3>Exam-style question</h3><p>Factorise 2x²−8 and solve 2x²−8=0. <strong>Solution:</strong> 2(x²−4)=2(x−2)(x+2), so x=<strong>2 or −2</strong>.</p><h3>Further study</h3><p>Continue to linear equations, quadratic equations, sequences and graphs using the current NERDC curriculum and approved mathematics texts.</p></div>"+T,
"<p><strong>Further practice:</strong> Expand (x+4)(x−3), factorise 3x²−12x, simplify 2⁵÷2² and √98.</p>"],

["SSS 1","Biology","Nutrition and Digestive Processes",
T+"<div class='senior-textbook'><h3>Nutrition and Digestive Processes</h3><p>This chapter explains classes of food, balanced diets, tests for food substances and the major stages of human digestion.</p><h3>Learning objectives</h3><p>Identify nutrients and their functions, explain balanced nutrition, describe food tests, trace digestion from mouth to large intestine and relate digestive structures to their functions.</p><h3>Classes of food</h3><table><tr><th>Nutrient</th><th>Main roles</th><th>Common sources</th></tr><tr><td>Carbohydrates</td><td>Primary energy source</td><td>Rice, yam, maize, bread</td></tr><tr><td>Proteins</td><td>Growth, repair and enzymes</td><td>Beans, eggs, fish, meat</td></tr><tr><td>Fats/oils</td><td>Energy reserve, insulation, membranes</td><td>Groundnut, oils, seeds</td></tr><tr><td>Vitamins</td><td>Support metabolic and protective functions</td><td>Fruits, vegetables and other foods</td></tr><tr><td>Minerals</td><td>Structural and regulatory roles</td><td>Milk, vegetables, meat, grains</td></tr><tr><td>Water</td><td>Solvent, transport and temperature regulation</td><td>Water and foods</td></tr><tr><td>Fibre</td><td>Supports normal bowel movement</td><td>Whole grains, vegetables, fruits</td></tr></table><h3>Balanced diet</h3><p>A balanced diet supplies required nutrients and energy in suitable amounts for the person's needs. Requirements vary with age, activity, physiological state and other factors. A meal is not balanced merely because it contains many different foods.</p><h3>Food tests</h3><p>Common school laboratory tests include iodine for starch, Benedict's reagent with appropriate heating for reducing sugars, Biuret test for protein, and an emulsion test for lipids. Use appropriate protective equipment and teacher-supervised procedures.</p><h3>Human digestion</h3><ol><li><strong>Mouth:</strong> chewing increases surface area; saliva begins starch digestion through amylase.</li><li><strong>Oesophagus:</strong> peristalsis moves food toward the stomach.</li><li><strong>Stomach:</strong> muscular mixing and acidic conditions support digestion, especially of proteins.</li><li><strong>Small intestine:</strong> bile assists fat emulsification; pancreatic and intestinal enzymes complete digestion; villi provide a large absorptive surface.</li><li><strong>Large intestine:</strong> absorbs much water and forms faeces.</li></ol><div class='box'><strong>Worked reasoning:</strong> If a student chews food thoroughly, the food is broken into smaller pieces, increasing surface area available to digestive enzymes. Chewing does not by itself complete chemical digestion.</div><h3>Practical activity</h3><p>Test labelled food samples for starch, reducing sugar and protein under teacher supervision. Record observations, controls and conclusions rather than merely writing the expected result.</p><h3>Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>Digestion is not the same as absorption.</li><li>Bile is not itself a digestive enzyme; it assists fat digestion physically by emulsification and contributes to suitable conditions.</li><li>Fibre is not simply a useless food component.</li></ul><h3>Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>State two functions of protein. <strong>Answer:</strong> growth and tissue repair; it is also used to make enzymes and other molecules.</li><li>Where is most nutrient absorption carried out? <strong>Answer:</strong> the small intestine.</li><li>What is peristalsis? <strong>Answer:</strong> coordinated muscular contractions that propel food through the alimentary canal.</li></ol><h3>Exam-style question</h3><p>Explain how the structure of a villus supports absorption. <strong>Answer:</strong> its large surface area, thin epithelial layer and rich blood supply facilitate efficient transfer of digested nutrients into circulation.</p><h3>Further study</h3><p>Study human nutrition, deficiency diseases and the detailed digestive-enzyme sequence in the current NERDC Biology curriculum.</p></div>"+T,
"<p><strong>Further practice:</strong> Draw and label the alimentary canal and create a table matching each major digestive organ with its principal function.</p>"],

["SSS 1","Chemistry","Mole Concept, Formulae and Chemical Calculations",
T+"<div class='senior-textbook'><h3>Mole Concept, Formulae and Chemical Calculations</h3><p>The mole provides a counting unit for particles and a bridge between microscopic particles and measurable laboratory quantities.</p><h3>Learning objectives</h3><p>Define relative atomic mass and mole, calculate molar mass, convert between mass and amount of substance, determine simple empirical formulae and use balanced equations in stoichiometric calculations.</p><h3>The mole</h3><p>One mole contains approximately 6.02×10²³ specified particles. The number is Avogadro's constant, N<sub>A</sub>. The molar mass has units g mol⁻¹ and is numerically equal to relative formula mass when expressed per mole.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> H₂O has relative formula mass 2(1)+16=<strong>18</strong>. Therefore 36 g of water represents 36/18=<strong>2.00 mol</strong>.</div><h3>Amount, mass and molar mass</h3><p>For a pure substance, <strong>n=m/M</strong>, where n is amount in mol, m is mass in g and M is molar mass in g mol⁻¹.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> Find the amount in 9.8 g H₂SO₄. M=2(1)+32+4(16)=98 g mol⁻¹. Therefore n=9.8/98=<strong>0.100 mol</strong>.</div><h3>Empirical formula</h3><p>The empirical formula gives the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms. Convert each element's mass to moles, divide all values by the smallest, then multiply by a suitable factor if necessary to obtain whole numbers.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> A compound contains 40.0% C, 6.67% H and 53.33% O. For 100 g: C≈3.33 mol, H≈6.67 mol, O≈3.33 mol. Divide by 3.33: approximately 1:2:1, giving <strong>CH₂O</strong>.</div><h3>Balanced equations and stoichiometry</h3><p>Coefficients in a balanced equation give mole ratios. For 2H₂+O₂→2H₂O, 2 mol H₂ react with 1 mol O₂ to form 2 mol H₂O, assuming complete reaction and appropriate conditions.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> If 0.50 mol O₂ reacts completely with excess H₂, the equation requires twice as many moles of H₂: <strong>1.00 mol H₂</strong>, producing <strong>1.00 mol H₂O</strong>.</div><h3>Practical activity</h3><p>Use a balance to measure a safe, teacher-approved substance, calculate its amount in moles and compare your calculated value with another group's result. Record uncertainty and significant figures.</p><h3>Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>A mole is not a mass unit; it is an amount-of-substance unit.</li><li>Molar mass is not always the same number as sample mass.</li><li>Balanced-equation coefficients represent mole ratios, not necessarily mass ratios.</li></ul><h3>Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>Find the molar mass of CO₂. <strong>Answer: 44 g mol⁻¹.</strong></li><li>How many moles are in 5.85 g NaCl (M=58.5 g mol⁻¹)? <strong>Answer: 0.100 mol.</strong></li><li>Find the empirical formula of a compound with ratio C:H:O=1:2:1. <strong>Answer: CH₂O.</strong></li></ol><h3>Exam-style question</h3><p>How many moles of water can form from 0.30 mol O₂ with excess hydrogen? From 2H₂+O₂→2H₂O, 1 mol O₂ gives 2 mol H₂O, so 0.30 mol gives <strong>0.60 mol H₂O</strong>.</p><h3>Further study</h3><p>Proceed to concentration, gas-volume relationships, limiting reagents and percentage yield with the current NERDC Chemistry curriculum.</p></div>"+T,
"<p><strong>Further practice:</strong> Calculate the molar masses of CaCO₃ and NH₄NO₃, then find the amount of substance in 10.0 g of each.</p>"],

["SSS 1","Physics","Electric Charge, Current and Simple Circuits",
T+"<div class='senior-textbook'><h3>Electric Charge, Current and Simple Circuits</h3><p>This chapter introduces charge, current, potential difference, resistance and safe construction of simple circuits.</p><h3>Learning objectives</h3><p>Distinguish charge and current, define potential difference and resistance, apply Ohm's law, interpret circuit diagrams and perform basic circuit measurements safely.</p><h3>Charge and current</h3><p>Electric charge is measured in coulombs (C). Electric current is the rate of flow of charge: <strong>I=Q/t</strong>, measured in amperes (A).</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> If 30 C passes a point in 5 s, I=30/5=<strong>6 A</strong>.</div><h3>Potential difference and resistance</h3><p>Potential difference is energy transferred per unit charge. Resistance measures opposition to current. For an ohmic conductor under suitable constant physical conditions, Ohm's law is <strong>V=IR</strong>.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> A 12 V supply is connected to a 4 Ω resistor. I=V/R=12/4=<strong>3 A</strong>.</div><h3>Series and parallel</h3><p>In a series circuit, current is the same through components and resistances add: R<sub>T</sub>=R₁+R₂+.... In parallel, the potential difference across branches is the same, while total current is the sum of branch currents. For two resistors in parallel, 1/R<sub>T</sub>=1/R₁+1/R₂.</p><div class='box'><strong>Worked example:</strong> 6 Ω and 3 Ω in parallel give 1/R=1/6+1/3=1/2, so R=<strong>2 Ω</strong>.</div><h3>Measuring circuits</h3><p>An ammeter is connected in series to measure current; a voltmeter is connected in parallel across the component whose potential difference is required. A multimeter must be set to an appropriate range and connection before measurement.</p><h3>Practical investigation</h3><p>Build a simple low-voltage circuit using a cell or approved power supply, switch and resistor. Measure V and I for several settings, tabulate results and plot V against I. Under conditions where Ohm's law applies, the graph is approximately linear and its gradient represents resistance.</p><h3>Safety</h3><ul><li>Use teacher-approved low-voltage equipment for school experiments.</li><li>Do not short-circuit a power source.</li><li>Switch off power before changing connections.</li><li>Inspect leads and equipment for damage.</li></ul><h3>Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>Current is not consumed by a resistor; energy is transferred.</li><li>A voltmeter is not placed in series in the standard measurement setup.</li><li>Ohm's law has conditions; not every component has a constant resistance.</li></ul><h3>Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>10 C passes in 2 s. Find current. <strong>Answer: 5 A.</strong></li><li>20 V across 5 Ω. Find current. <strong>Answer: 4 A.</strong></li><li>Two 4 Ω resistors in series. Find total resistance. <strong>Answer: 8 Ω.</strong></li><li>Two 4 Ω resistors in parallel. Find total resistance. <strong>Answer: 2 Ω.</strong></li></ol><h3>Exam-style question</h3><p>A 24 V source supplies a 6 Ω resistor. Calculate current and power. I=24/6=4 A; P=VI=24×4=<strong>96 W</strong>.</p><h3>Further study</h3><p>Continue to electrical energy, power, household circuits, magnetism and electromagnetic induction using the current NERDC Physics curriculum.</p></div>"+T,
"<p><strong>Further practice:</strong> Draw a series circuit containing two resistors and a switch; then draw a parallel arrangement and explain one practical difference between them.</p>"],
,
["SSS 1","Agriculture","Soil Formation, Properties and Fertility","\n<div class=\"senior-textbook\">\n<h3>1. Learning objectives</h3><p>By the end of this chapter, the learner should explain how soil forms, identify major soil components, distinguish common soil types, test important properties, and explain practical ways of maintaining soil fertility.</p>\n<h3>2. Meaning and importance of soil</h3><p>Soil is the relatively loose material at the earth's surface in which plants can grow. It provides anchorage, water, air and mineral nutrients and supports organisms involved in decomposition and nutrient cycling. For Nigerian agriculture, soil management is closely linked to crop choice, yield, erosion control and sustainable land use.</p>\n<h3>3. Soil formation</h3><p>Soil develops over long periods through the interaction of <strong>parent material, climate, organisms, relief and time</strong>. Weathering breaks rocks into smaller particles. Organic remains are decomposed and mixed with mineral particles, while movement of water and organisms changes the developing soil profile.</p>\n<h3>4. Soil components</h3><table><tr><th>Component</th><th>Role</th></tr><tr><td>Mineral particles</td><td>Provide the mineral framework and influence texture.</td></tr><tr><td>Organic matter</td><td>Improves structure and contributes nutrients as it decomposes.</td></tr><tr><td>Water</td><td>Dissolves and transports nutrients and supports biological reactions.</td></tr><tr><td>Air</td><td>Supplies oxygen for roots and soil organisms.</td></tr><tr><td>Living organisms</td><td>Decompose materials, form pores and participate in nutrient cycling.</td></tr></table>\n<h3>5. Soil texture and types</h3><p>Texture describes the relative proportions of sand, silt and clay. <strong>Sandy soil</strong> has large particles and usually drains quickly. <strong>Clay soil</strong> has very small particles, holds much water and may drain slowly. <strong>Loam</strong> contains a useful balance of particle sizes and is often favourable for many crops when well managed.</p>\n<h3>6. Soil structure, porosity and drainage</h3><p>Structure describes how particles are arranged into aggregates. Good structure creates pores for air and water while allowing roots to penetrate. Compaction reduces pore spaces and can restrict roots and drainage.</p>\n<h3>7. Soil fertility</h3><p>Soil fertility is the capacity of soil to supply essential nutrients in suitable amounts and forms. Major plant nutrients include nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium. Fertility can be maintained through appropriate organic manures, suitable fertilisers, crop rotation, erosion control, residue management and soil testing.</p>\n<h3>8. Practical investigation</h3><div class=\"box\"><strong>Jar texture test:</strong> Put a soil sample in a transparent jar with water, shake thoroughly and allow it to settle. Larger particles settle first and finer particles later. Observe the layers. This gives an approximate indication of texture, not a laboratory-grade classification.</div><p><strong>Safety:</strong> Wash hands after handling soil and do not inhale dust. Do not taste samples or chemicals.</p>\n<h3>9. Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>Fertile soil is not simply soil that looks dark.</li><li>Adding more fertiliser is not always better; excessive application can damage crops and water systems.</li><li>All clay soils are not identical; structure, organic matter and drainage also matter.</li></ul>\n<h3>10. Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>Name four components of soil.</li><li>State two differences between sandy and clay soils.</li><li>Why is organic matter important?</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Mineral particles, organic matter, water and air (among others). 2. Sandy soil has larger particles and generally drains faster; clay has finer particles and generally retains more water. 3. It contributes nutrients, improves aggregation and supports biological activity.</div>\n<h3>11. Examination practice</h3><p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain three factors that can reduce soil fertility and give one management measure for each.</p><p><strong>Model answer:</strong> Nutrient removal by repeated cropping can be reduced by appropriate fertilisation or rotation; erosion can be reduced by cover crops, mulching or erosion-control practices; organic-matter loss can be reduced by returning suitable organic residues or manure to the soil.</p>\n</div>"],
["SSS 1","Geography","Maps, Scale, Direction and Geographical Skills","\n<div class=\"senior-textbook\">\n<h3>1. Learning objectives</h3><p>Interpret maps, calculate and use scale, determine direction, use conventional symbols and distinguish grid-based measurements from visual estimates.</p>\n<h3>2. Meaning of a map</h3><p>A map is a reduced, selected and conventional representation of part of the earth's surface. Because a map cannot show everything, the cartographer selects information according to purpose and scale.</p>\n<h3>3. Scale</h3><p>Scale expresses the relationship between a distance on a map and the corresponding ground distance. It may be written as a ratio, statement or line scale.</p><div class=\"box\"><strong>Worked example:</strong> On a 1:50,000 map, 1 cm represents 50,000 cm on the ground. Since 100,000 cm = 1 km, 50,000 cm = 0.5 km. Therefore 6 cm represents 3 km.</div>\n<h3>4. Direction and bearings</h3><p>The cardinal directions are north, east, south and west, with intermediate directions such as northeast. A three-figure bearing is measured clockwise from north and written from 000° to 360°. Thus east is 090° and south is 180°.</p>\n<h3>5. Conventional signs and symbols</h3><p>Maps use standardised or explained symbols for features such as roads, rivers, settlements and boundaries. Always consult the legend before interpreting an unfamiliar symbol.</p>\n<h3>6. Grid references</h3><p>Grid references locate features using numbered grid lines. In the common rule <strong>eastings first, northings second</strong>, read across before reading upward. The precision of a reference depends on the number of digits required by the map.</p>\n<h3>7. Relief and contour lines</h3><p>Contour lines join points of equal elevation. Close contours indicate a steep slope; widely spaced contours indicate a gentler slope. The contour interval must be identified before calculating height differences.</p>\n<h3>8. Practical activity</h3><p>Using a suitable school topographic map, identify five conventional signs, calculate the ground distance between two features, determine one bearing and describe the relief along a selected route. Record every calculation and unit.</p>\n<h3>9. Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>A larger map scale such as 1:10,000 shows more detail than 1:100,000 because the representative fraction is larger.</li><li>Bearings are measured clockwise from north, not from the nearest road.</li><li>Contour lines normally do not cross one another because a point cannot ordinarily have two different elevations.</li></ul>\n<h3>10. Exercises</h3><ol><li>What ground distance does 8 cm represent on a 1:25,000 map?</li><li>State the bearing of due west.</li><li>What does a close contour spacing suggest?</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. 8 × 25,000 = 200,000 cm = 2 km. 2. 270°. 3. A relatively steep slope.</div>\n<h3>11. Examination practice</h3><p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain how scale, direction and grid references work together when planning a journey from one mapped settlement to another.</p><p><strong>Model answer:</strong> Scale converts map distance to ground distance; direction or bearing gives the travel orientation; and grid references identify the precise locations of the starting point and destination. A complete response should also consider mapped relief, roads and other relevant features.</p>\n</div>"],
["SSS 1","Technical Drawing","Drawing Instruments, Lines and Geometric Constructions","\n<div class=\"senior-textbook\">\n<h3>1. Learning objectives</h3><p>Identify common technical-drawing instruments, use them correctly, distinguish line types and construct basic geometric figures accurately.</p>\n<h3>2. Drawing instruments</h3><table><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Typical use</th></tr><tr><td>T-square</td><td>Drawing horizontal lines and supporting set squares.</td></tr><tr><td>Set squares</td><td>Drawing standard angles such as 30°, 45°, 60° and 90°.</td></tr><tr><td>Compass</td><td>Drawing circles and arcs.</td></tr><tr><td>Divider</td><td>Transferring or comparing distances.</td></tr><tr><td>Protractor</td><td>Measuring or setting angles.</td></tr><tr><td>Scale rule</td><td>Reading or producing scaled drawings.</td></tr></table>\n<h3>3. Line conventions</h3><p>Different line types communicate different information. Continuous thick lines commonly show visible outlines; thin construction lines support accurate construction; dashed lines may indicate hidden detail; and centre lines identify axes or centres. The exact convention should follow the applicable school/technical drawing standard.</p>\n<h3>4. Geometric construction: perpendicular bisector</h3><ol><li>Draw the line segment AB.</li><li>With the compass centred at A, draw arcs above and below AB with radius greater than half AB.</li><li>Using the same radius centred at B, draw arcs crossing the first pair.</li><li>Join the arc intersections. The resulting line bisects AB at 90°.</li></ol>\n<h3>5. Construction of a 60° angle</h3><p>With the vertex as centre, draw an arc cutting one arm. With the same radius, place the compass on the intersection and draw a second arc. Join the vertex to the second intersection. The included angle is 60°.</p>\n<h3>6. Accuracy and care</h3><p>Keep pencils sharp, use light construction lines, avoid excessive pressure, keep instruments clean and check measurements before darkening final outlines.</p>\n<h3>7. Practical project</h3><p>Construct a triangle from given side lengths, draw its perpendicular bisectors and angle bisectors, and label each construction line. Submit the final drawing with a title and appropriate line quality.</p>\n<h3>8. Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>A freehand estimate is not a geometric construction.</li><li>Construction lines should not normally be darkened as if they were final outlines.</li><li>Instrument accuracy depends on correct use and careful measurement.</li></ul>\n<h3>9. Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>What is a compass used for?</li><li>Why are construction lines kept light?</li><li>State one use of a set square.</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Circles and arcs. 2. They guide construction and should remain distinguishable from final outlines. 3. Drawing standard angles or perpendicular/parallel lines.</div>\n<h3>10. Examination practice</h3><p><strong>Task:</strong> Construct the perpendicular bisector of a 70 mm line segment and explain each step. Marks should reward accurate construction, correct compass use, clear lines and labels.</p>\n</div>"],
["SSS 1","Physical Education","Physical Fitness, Health and Wellness","\n<div class=\"senior-textbook\">\n<h3>1. Learning objectives</h3><p>Define physical fitness, identify major components of fitness, explain basic training principles and design a safe simple fitness routine.</p>\n<h3>2. Meaning of physical fitness</h3><p>Physical fitness is the ability to perform daily activities and physical tasks effectively, with sufficient reserve and without undue fatigue. Fitness is multidimensional rather than simply being able to run fast.</p>\n<h3>3. Components</h3><table><tr><th>Component</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>Cardiorespiratory endurance</td><td>Ability to sustain aerobic activity.</td><td>Jogging.</td></tr><tr><td>Muscular strength</td><td>Ability to exert force.</td><td>Controlled resistance exercise.</td></tr><tr><td>Muscular endurance</td><td>Ability to repeat or sustain muscular work.</td><td>Repeated body-weight movements.</td></tr><tr><td>Flexibility</td><td>Range of movement at joints.</td><td>Appropriate stretching.</td></tr><tr><td>Body composition</td><td>Relative amounts of body tissues.</td><td>Assessed with appropriate measures.</td></tr></table>\n<h3>4. Training principles</h3><p><strong>Overload</strong> means giving the body a greater challenge than its accustomed load. <strong>Progression</strong> increases the challenge gradually. <strong>Specificity</strong> means training adaptations relate to the activity trained. Rest and recovery are necessary components of safe training.</p>\n<h3>5. Warm-up and cool-down</h3><p>A warm-up prepares the body and mind for activity through progressively suitable movement. A cool-down provides a gradual transition toward resting activity. Neither removes all risk of injury, so technique, equipment and appropriate supervision remain important.</p>\n<h3>6. Practical activity</h3><p>Record a simple school fitness circuit such as brisk walking, controlled squats, marching and mobility exercises. Record duration and perceived effort, allow rest, and compare performance over several weeks rather than attempting unsafe maximal tests.</p>\n<h3>7. Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>Sweating alone does not prove that a workout was effective.</li><li>More exercise is not automatically better; recovery and appropriateness matter.</li><li>Fitness should be developed progressively, especially after inactivity.</li></ul>\n<h3>8. Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>Name four components of fitness.</li><li>Explain progression.</li><li>Why is warm-up useful?</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Cardiorespiratory endurance, strength, muscular endurance and flexibility. 2. Gradually increasing training demand as adaptation occurs. 3. It prepares the body for activity and helps the learner transition into the intended movement safely.</div>\n</div>"],
["SSS 1","Health Education","Personal Hygiene and Healthy Living","\n<div class=\"senior-textbook\">\n<h3>1. Learning objectives</h3><p>Explain personal hygiene, identify practices that reduce infection risk and connect hygiene with healthy living without confusing hygiene with every aspect of health.</p>\n<h3>2. Personal hygiene</h3><p>Personal hygiene refers to practices that help maintain cleanliness and reduce exposure to harmful microorganisms and contaminants. Examples include appropriate hand hygiene, oral care, bathing, clean clothing and safe handling of personal items.</p>\n<h3>3. Hand hygiene</h3><p>Hands can transfer microorganisms from contaminated surfaces to the mouth, nose, eyes, food and other people. Wash hands with soap and safe running water when appropriate, especially after using the toilet, before preparing or eating food and after activities that visibly contaminate the hands.</p>\n<h3>4. Oral hygiene</h3><p>Regular toothbrushing with fluoride toothpaste, cleaning between teeth where appropriate, limiting frequent exposure to sugary foods and beverages, and seeking dental care when needed contribute to oral health.</p>\n<h3>5. Personal items and environment</h3><p>Items such as toothbrushes, razors and some cosmetics should not be shared where sharing creates infection or contamination risks. Clean surroundings, safe water, sanitation and proper waste management support—but do not replace—personal hygiene.</p>\n<h3>6. Practical demonstration</h3><p>Conduct a supervised handwashing demonstration. Identify commonly missed areas such as thumbs, fingertips and spaces between fingers. Discuss when hand hygiene is particularly important and how clean water and soap availability affect practice.</p>\n<h3>7. Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>Good hygiene reduces many risks but cannot prevent every disease.</li><li>Strong-smelling products do not necessarily mean better hygiene.</li><li>Antibiotics are not a substitute for hygiene and should not be used casually for viral illnesses.</li></ul>\n<h3>8. Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>Define personal hygiene.</li><li>Give three situations requiring hand hygiene.</li><li>Why should personal items sometimes not be shared?</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Practices that maintain cleanliness and reduce exposure to contaminants and harmful microorganisms. 2. Before eating/preparing food, after toilet use, and after contamination. 3. Sharing can transfer microorganisms or bodily fluids.</div>\n</div>"],
["SSS 1","Foods & Nutrition","Food Groups, Nutrients and Balanced Diet","\n<div class=\"senior-textbook\">\n<h3>1. Learning objectives</h3><p>Classify major nutrients, state their functions and sources, explain a balanced diet and apply nutrition principles to realistic meals.</p>\n<h3>2. Major nutrients</h3><table><tr><th>Nutrient</th><th>Main functions</th><th>Examples of sources</th></tr><tr><td>Carbohydrate</td><td>Major energy source.</td><td>Cereals, roots, tubers, fruits.</td></tr><tr><td>Protein</td><td>Growth, repair and production of body substances.</td><td>Beans, eggs, fish, meat, milk, nuts.</td></tr><tr><td>Fat</td><td>Energy, insulation and absorption of fat-soluble vitamins.</td><td>Oils, seeds, nuts and animal sources.</td></tr><tr><td>Vitamins</td><td>Support many body processes.</td><td>Fruits, vegetables and other varied foods.</td></tr><tr><td>Minerals</td><td>Structural and regulatory roles.</td><td>Varied foods, depending on mineral.</td></tr><tr><td>Water</td><td>Transport, temperature regulation and metabolic functions.</td><td>Drinking water and water-rich foods.</td></tr></table>\n<h3>3. Balanced diet</h3><p>A balanced diet supplies adequate amounts and variety of nutrients for an individual's needs. Requirements vary with age, sex, activity, physiological state and health circumstances. Balance is about overall dietary pattern, not one “perfect” meal.</p>\n<h3>4. Meal planning example</h3><div class=\"box\"><strong>Scenario:</strong> A student plans rice, beans, vegetables, fish and fruit with water. The combination supplies carbohydrate, protein, fibre, micronutrients and fluids. The exact portion sizes should depend on the person's needs; the example is a planning exercise, not a medical prescription.</div>\n<h3>5. Deficiency and excess</h3><p>Inadequate intake of particular nutrients can contribute to deficiency disorders, while excessive intake of energy or some nutrients can also contribute to health problems. Nutrition education should therefore emphasise variety, adequacy, moderation and food safety.</p>\n<h3>6. Practical activity</h3><p>Choose a typical Nigerian meal and make a table identifying its likely nutrient contributions. Suggest two additions or substitutions that improve variety while remaining affordable and culturally appropriate.</p>\n<h3>7. Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>“Carbohydrate is bad” is an oversimplification; carbohydrate-containing foods vary greatly in nutrient content.</li><li>One food rarely supplies every nutrient in adequate amounts.</li><li>Healthy eating does not require expensive imported foods.</li></ul>\n<h3>8. Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>State two functions of protein.</li><li>Why is water a nutrient?</li><li>What does a balanced diet mean?</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Growth/repair and production of body substances. 2. It performs essential functions including transport and temperature regulation. 3. A dietary pattern that provides adequate and varied nutrients appropriate to the person's needs.</div>\n</div>"],
["SSS 1","Literature in English","Introduction to Literary Genres and Devices","\n<div class=\"senior-textbook\">\n<h3>1. Learning objectives</h3><p>Distinguish major literary genres, identify common literary devices and support interpretations with evidence from a text.</p>\n<h3>2. Literature and genres</h3><p>Literature uses language creatively to explore experiences, ideas, conflicts and human relationships. The major genres are <strong>poetry, drama and prose</strong>. A work may also combine techniques across genres.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Genre</th><th>Important features to study</th></tr><tr><td>Poetry</td><td>Voice, form, rhythm, imagery, diction, sound and structure.</td></tr><tr><td>Drama</td><td>Dialogue, action, character, conflict, stage directions and performance.</td></tr><tr><td>Prose</td><td>Narrator, plot, setting, characterisation, style, point of view and structure.</td></tr></table>\n<h3>3. Literary devices</h3><p><strong>Simile</strong> makes an explicit comparison, often using “like” or “as.” <strong>Metaphor</strong> presents one thing in terms of another. <strong>Personification</strong> gives human qualities to non-human things. <strong>Irony</strong> involves a significant contrast between appearance/expectation and meaning or outcome. <strong>Symbolism</strong> uses an element to suggest broader meaning.</p>\n<h3>4. How to analyse a passage</h3><ol><li>Read for literal meaning first.</li><li>Identify who is speaking or narrating and what is happening.</li><li>Mark important words, images, repetitions and contrasts.</li><li>Ask what effect each feature produces.</li><li>Connect the feature to theme, character, mood or structure.</li><li>Quote only enough text to support the point and explain it.</li></ol>\n<h3>5. Original mini-example</h3><div class=\"box\"><strong>Example:</strong> “The classroom swallowed the noise when the teacher entered.” The verb “swallowed” is a metaphor/personifying image. It suggests that the noise disappeared suddenly and helps create a vivid picture of silence. The interpretation should be tied to context rather than treated as a dictionary definition.</div>\n<h3>6. Practical activity</h3><p>Select a short public-domain or teacher-approved passage. Highlight three devices, identify their immediate effects, and write one paragraph connecting one device to a theme or character.</p>\n<h3>7. Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>Not every unusual phrase is a metaphor.</li><li>Identifying a device is only the first step; literary analysis must explain its effect and significance.</li><li>There can be more than one defensible interpretation when supported by textual evidence.</li></ul>\n<h3>8. Examination practice</h3><p><strong>Question:</strong> Why is textual evidence important in literary analysis?</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> It anchors an interpretation in what the text actually says or does. A strong response identifies evidence, explains its effect and connects it to the question instead of merely asserting an opinion.</p>\n</div>"],
["SSS 1","Home Management","Family Resource Management","\n<div class=\"senior-textbook\">\n<h3>1. Learning objectives</h3><p>Explain family resources, distinguish needs from wants, plan a simple household budget and describe responsible resource use.</p>\n<h3>2. Family resources</h3><p>Resources include <strong>human resources</strong> such as time, knowledge, skills and labour, and <strong>material/non-human resources</strong> such as money, equipment, housing, water and energy. Resources are limited, so families make choices about their use.</p>\n<h3>3. Needs and wants</h3><p>Needs are necessities required for basic wellbeing, while wants are desired goods or services beyond immediate necessities. The distinction can depend on circumstances; for example, transport may be a need for a worker whose livelihood depends on reaching a workplace.</p>\n<h3>4. Simple household budget</h3><div class=\"box\"><strong>Worked example:</strong> Suppose a household has ₦120,000 available for a month. Planned expenses are food ₦45,000, transport ₦20,000, education ₦15,000, utilities ₦12,000 and savings ₦10,000. Total planned allocation = ₦102,000. Unallocated balance = ₦18,000. The balance should not automatically be treated as disposable spending because emergencies and irregular expenses may arise.</div>\n<h3>5. Decision-making</h3><p>Good household management involves setting priorities, comparing alternatives, considering opportunity cost, recording spending and reviewing outcomes. A budget is a plan, not a guarantee that actual spending will equal the estimate.</p>\n<h3>6. Practical activity</h3><p>Create a fictional monthly budget for a student or small household. Separate fixed and variable expenses, identify one saving target, and explain two trade-offs made in the plan. Do not collect classmates' private financial information.</p>\n<h3>7. Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>A budget is not simply a list of purchases.</li><li>Saving is a planned allocation, not money that appears automatically at month end.</li><li>The cheapest item is not always the lowest-cost choice if durability or quality differs substantially.</li></ul>\n<h3>8. Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>Give two examples of human resources.</li><li>What is opportunity cost?</li><li>If income is ₦80,000 and planned expenses are ₦73,500, what is the planned balance?</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Time, skills, knowledge or labour. 2. The next-best alternative forgone when a choice is made. 3. ₦6,500.</div>\n</div>"],
["SSS 1","Digital Technologies","Computer Networks and Internet Fundamentals","<div class=\"senior-textbook\"><h3>1. Learning objectives</h3><p>Explain what a computer network is, distinguish common network types and devices, describe how data moves across networks, and apply safe internet practices.</p><h3>2. Network basics</h3><p>A computer network connects devices so they can communicate and share resources. A <strong>LAN</strong> covers a relatively small area such as a school or office; a <strong>WAN</strong> spans larger geographical areas. The Internet is a global interconnection of networks.</p><h3>3. Common devices</h3><table><tr><th>Device</th><th>Function</th></tr><tr><td>Switch</td><td>Connects devices within a local network and forwards frames to appropriate ports.</td></tr><tr><td>Router</td><td>Connects different networks and forwards packets between them.</td></tr><tr><td>Access point</td><td>Provides wireless network access.</td></tr><tr><td>Modem</td><td>Provides the interface/modulation function needed by some access technologies.</td></tr></table><h3>4. Addresses and protocols</h3><p>An IP address identifies a network interface for communication at the Internet Protocol layer. Protocols are agreed rules for communication. HTTP/HTTPS are used for web communication, while DNS translates domain names into IP addresses.</p><h3>5. Practical activity</h3><p>Draw a school network containing computers, a switch, a router and an Internet connection. Label the path a request might take from a student computer to a web server. Do not record passwords or private network credentials.</p><h3>6. Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>Wi-Fi is not the same thing as the Internet; Wi-Fi is a wireless networking method.</li><li>A router and a switch do different jobs.</li><li>A website address is not itself a password.</li></ul><h3>7. Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>What is a LAN?</li><li>State one function of a router.</li><li>What does DNS do?</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. A local area network. 2. It forwards traffic between networks. 3. It resolves domain names to network addresses.</div></div>"],
["SSS 1","Digital Technologies","Information Search, Evaluation and Digital Literacy","<div class=\"senior-textbook\"><h3>1. Learning objectives</h3><p>Find information efficiently, distinguish evidence from unsupported claims, evaluate online sources and cite information responsibly.</p><h3>2. Search strategy</h3><p>Start with precise keywords. Use quotation marks for exact phrases when appropriate, add context terms, and refine a search when results are too broad. A search engine result is a lead to a source, not proof that the source is correct.</p><h3>3. Evaluating sources</h3><table><tr><th>Question</th><th>What to check</th></tr><tr><td>Who?</td><td>Author, institution and expertise.</td></tr><tr><td>When?</td><td>Publication/update date and whether currency matters.</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence?</td><td>References, data, methodology and primary documentation.</td></tr><tr><td>Purpose?</td><td>Education, reporting, advertising, persuasion or entertainment.</td></tr></table><h3>4. Triangulation</h3><p>For important claims, compare authoritative or primary sources and check whether they actually refer to the same population, date and definition. Do not treat three websites repeating the same unsupported claim as three independent confirmations.</p><h3>5. Practical research task</h3><p>Choose a school topic. Find one official source, one reputable educational source and one additional source. Record author/institution, date, key claim and evidence. Identify any disagreement and explain why one source may be more authoritative for that specific claim.</p><h3>6. Digital ethics</h3><p>Respect copyright, privacy and attribution. Do not copy another person's work and present it as your own. Avoid publishing another person's private information without a legitimate reason and appropriate permission.</p><h3>7. Exercises</h3><ol><li>Why is publication date important?</li><li>What is triangulation?</li><li>Why is a search result not automatically evidence?</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Some facts become outdated. 2. Comparing independent relevant sources to test a claim. 3. The result only points to a source; the source itself must be evaluated.</div></div>"],
["SSS 1","Digital Technologies","Cybersecurity, Privacy, Safety and Digital Ethics","<div class=\"senior-textbook\"><h3>1. Learning objectives</h3><p>Identify common cyber risks, practise safer account and device habits, protect personal information and recognise ethical responsibilities online.</p><h3>2. Core risks</h3><p><strong>Phishing</strong> attempts to trick people into revealing information or taking unsafe actions. Malware is malicious software. Social engineering manipulates people rather than merely exploiting technical weaknesses. Common warning signs include unexpected urgency, suspicious links, requests for secrets and unusual payment demands.</p><h3>3. Safer accounts</h3><p>Use strong, unique passwords or passphrases and enable multi-factor authentication where available. Never share one-time codes or passwords with someone who asks unexpectedly. Keep operating systems and applications updated from legitimate sources.</p><h3>4. Privacy</h3><p>Personal information can include names, contact details, credentials, location information and identifying records. Before posting, ask whether the information is necessary, who can access it and how it could be misused.</p><h3>5. Incident response</h3><ol><li>Stop interacting with a suspicious message.</li><li>Preserve useful evidence such as the message or transaction record.</li><li>Change compromised credentials from a trusted device when appropriate.</li><li>Report the incident to the relevant platform, school, bank or authority.</li></ol><h3>6. Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>Antivirus software cannot replace careful behaviour.</li><li>Knowing someone's name does not give permission to access their account.</li><li>Private accounts can still expose information through screenshots or compromised credentials.</li></ul><h3>7. Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>Name two signs of phishing.</li><li>Why should passwords be unique?</li><li>What is multi-factor authentication?</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Unexpected urgency and suspicious links, among others. 2. A breach of one service should not automatically expose other accounts. 3. Authentication requiring more than one independent factor.</div></div>"],
["SSS 1","Commerce","Trade, Occupations and Aids to Trade","<div class=\"senior-textbook\"><h3>1. Learning objectives</h3><p>Explain trade, distinguish home and foreign trade, classify occupations and describe services that make trade easier.</p><h3>2. Trade</h3><p>Trade involves buying and selling goods and services. <strong>Home trade</strong> takes place within a country, while <strong>foreign trade</strong> involves transactions across national borders.</p><h3>3. Occupations</h3><p>Occupation refers to a person's regular work or economic activity. Common broad categories include extractive, manufacturing, construction, commercial and service activities. One person may participate in more than one economic activity over time.</p><h3>4. Aids to trade</h3><table><tr><th>Aid</th><th>Contribution</th></tr><tr><td>Transport</td><td>Moves people and goods.</td></tr><tr><td>Warehousing</td><td>Stores goods until needed.</td></tr><tr><td>Banking</td><td>Supports payments, savings and finance.</td></tr><tr><td>Insurance</td><td>Transfers specified financial risks under a contract.</td></tr><tr><td>Communication</td><td>Transmits information needed for transactions.</td></tr></table><h3>5. Application</h3><p>Consider a farmer selling packaged vegetables to an urban retailer. Production creates the goods; transport moves them; packaging and storage protect them; communication coordinates the order; banking facilitates payment; and appropriate insurance may manage specified risks.</p><h3>6. Exercises</h3><ol><li>Define trade.</li><li>Give three aids to trade.</li><li>Differentiate home and foreign trade.</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Buying and selling goods and services. 2. Transport, banking and warehousing, among others. 3. Home trade is within a country; foreign trade crosses national borders.</div></div>"],
["SSS 1","Accounting","Books of Original Entry","<div class=\"senior-textbook\"><h3>1. Learning objectives</h3><p>Explain why books of original entry are used, identify common source documents and determine the appropriate book for common transactions.</p><h3>2. Meaning</h3><p>Books of original entry are books or records in which transactions are first recorded from source documents before information is posted to the ledger, depending on the accounting system.</p><h3>3. Common records</h3><table><tr><th>Book</th><th>Typical transactions</th></tr><tr><td>Sales day book</td><td>Credit sales of goods.</td></tr><tr><td>Purchases day book</td><td>Credit purchases of goods.</td></tr><tr><td>Returns inward book</td><td>Goods returned by credit customers.</td></tr><tr><td>Returns outward book</td><td>Goods returned to credit suppliers.</td></tr><tr><td>Cash book</td><td>Cash and bank transactions.</td></tr><tr><td>Journal proper</td><td>Transactions requiring journal entries, subject to the accounting system.</td></tr></table><h3>4. Source documents</h3><p>Invoices, receipts, credit notes, debit notes, vouchers and related records provide evidence for transactions. The exact document depends on the transaction.</p><h3>5. Worked classification</h3><div class=\"box\">A shop sells goods on credit to a customer: record the transaction in the <strong>sales day book</strong>. A customer returns goods previously bought on credit: use the <strong>returns inward book</strong>. Cash received from a customer is recorded through the appropriate <strong>cash book</strong> entry.</div><h3>6. Practical activity</h3><p>Create fictional source documents and classify each transaction into the appropriate book. Use invented names and amounts rather than classmates' or real customers' private information.</p><h3>7. Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>What is a source document?</li><li>Which book normally records credit purchases of goods?</li><li>Which book records cash transactions?</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Documentary evidence supporting a transaction. 2. Purchases day book. 3. Cash book.</div></div>"],
["SSS 1","Marketing","Meaning and Scope of Marketing","<div class=\"senior-textbook\"><h3>1. Learning objectives</h3><p>Define marketing, distinguish it from selling and identify the activities through which organisations create, communicate and deliver value to customers.</p><h3>2. Meaning of marketing</h3><p>Marketing is the process of understanding customer needs and coordinating activities that create, communicate, deliver and exchange offerings of value. Selling is one part of marketing, not its entire meaning.</p><h3>3. Marketing activities</h3><ul><li>Researching customers and markets.</li><li>Designing or selecting suitable products/services.</li><li>Branding and packaging where appropriate.</li><li>Setting and communicating prices.</li><li>Selecting distribution channels.</li><li>Promotion and customer relationship management.</li></ul><h3>4. Customer value</h3><p>Customers consider benefits, quality, convenience, price, trust and other factors. Marketing decisions therefore involve trade-offs and should not rely on misleading claims.</p><h3>5. Practical scenario</h3><div class=\"box\">A small Nigerian bakery notices that commuters want affordable breakfast items available early. The owner can research demand, design suitable packs, choose a price, identify distribution points and communicate accurate information. Each decision should be tested against actual customer response rather than assumed.</div><h3>6. Ethics</h3><p>Marketing should respect consumers, avoid deceptive claims and comply with applicable rules. Accurate product information is especially important for foods, health-related products and financial services.</p><h3>7. Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>Define marketing.</li><li>Why is marketing broader than selling?</li><li>Name four marketing activities.</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Coordinated activities for understanding markets and creating, communicating, delivering and exchanging value. 2. It includes research, product, pricing, distribution and communication decisions. 3. Research, product planning, pricing and promotion, among others.</div></div>"],
["SSS 1","Economics","Elasticity of Demand and Supply","<div class=\"senior-textbook\"><h3>1. Learning objectives</h3><p>Define elasticity, calculate price elasticity of demand from percentage changes and interpret whether demand is relatively elastic or inelastic.</p><h3>2. Price elasticity of demand</h3><p>Price elasticity of demand measures the responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price, other things being equal.</p><div class=\"box\"><strong>Formula:</strong> PED = percentage change in quantity demanded ÷ percentage change in price.</div><h3>3. Worked example</h3><p>Suppose price rises by 10% and quantity demanded falls by 20%. PED = −20% ÷ 10% = <strong>−2</strong>. The negative sign reflects the usual inverse price-demand relationship. In magnitude, 2 indicates relatively elastic demand in this example.</p><h3>4. Determinants</h3><p>Responsiveness can be influenced by availability of substitutes, whether the good is a necessity or luxury, the proportion of income spent on it, time available to adjust and how narrowly the product is defined.</p><h3>5. Supply elasticity</h3><p>Price elasticity of supply measures the responsiveness of quantity supplied to a change in price. Producers may respond more strongly when they have spare capacity or enough time to adjust inputs.</p><h3>6. Practical graphing</h3><p>Use a table of fictional price and quantity data. Calculate percentage changes consistently and discuss whether the resulting elasticity suggests a strong or weak quantity response.</p><h3>7. Exercises and answers</h3><ol><li>What does PED measure?</li><li>If quantity demanded falls 5% after price rises 10%, calculate PED.</li><li>Name two determinants of elasticity.</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Responsiveness of quantity demanded to price changes. 2. −5% ÷ 10% = <strong>−0.5</strong>. 3. Availability of substitutes and time to adjust, among others.</div></div>"],
["SSS 1","Further Mathematics","Sequences, Series and Mathematical Induction","<div class=\"senior-textbook\"><h3>1. Learning objectives</h3><p>Distinguish sequences from series, identify arithmetic and geometric patterns, use their basic formulae and understand the purpose of mathematical induction.</p><h3>2. Sequences and series</h3><p>A <strong>sequence</strong> is an ordered list of terms. A <strong>series</strong> is formed by adding terms of a sequence.</p><h3>3. Arithmetic progression</h3><p>An arithmetic progression has a constant common difference <em>d</em>. Its nth term is <strong>a<sub>n</sub> = a + (n − 1)d</strong>.</p><div class=\"box\"><strong>Worked example:</strong> For 5, 8, 11, 14, ... we have a = 5 and d = 3. The 10th term is 5 + 9(3) = <strong>32</strong>.</div><h3>4. Geometric progression</h3><p>A geometric progression has a constant common ratio <em>r</em>. Its nth term is <strong>a<sub>n</sub> = ar<sup>n−1</sup></strong>.</p><h3>5. Mathematical induction</h3><p>Induction is a proof method for statements concerning positive integers. A typical proof has a <strong>base case</strong>, followed by an <strong>inductive step</strong> showing that if the statement holds for an arbitrary integer k, it also holds for k+1.</p><h3>6. Practice</h3><ol><li>Find the 15th term of 2, 5, 8, ...</li><li>Identify the common ratio in 3, 6, 12, 24, ...</li><li>Why is the base case necessary in induction?</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. 2 + 14(3) = <strong>44</strong>. 2. 2. 3. It establishes that the statement is true at the starting value.</div></div>"],
["SSS 1","Christian Religious Studies","Creation, Human Nature and Responsibility","<div class=\"senior-textbook\"><h3>Learning objectives</h3><p>Explain major creation themes in Genesis, discuss human responsibility and distinguish textual interpretation from personal application.</p><h3>Creation narratives</h3><p>Genesis 1 presents creation in an ordered sequence, while Genesis 2 provides a complementary account focused more closely on the human being, the garden and responsibility. In classroom study, compare what each passage emphasises rather than forcing every detail into an identical structure.</p><h3>Human responsibility</h3><p>The texts connect human life with stewardship, obedience, work, relationships and accountability. Stewardship involves responsible use of creation rather than unrestricted exploitation.</p><h3>Interpretive method</h3><ol><li>Read the passage in context.</li><li>Identify repeated words, actions and contrasts.</li><li>Ask what the passage explicitly states.</li><li>Distinguish textual observation from interpretation.</li><li>Relate the teaching to responsible conduct.</li></ol><h3>Exercises</h3><ol><li>State two features of Genesis 1.</li><li>What does stewardship mean in this context?</li><li>Why should textual observation be separated from interpretation?</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Ordered creation and repeated declarations of God's creative work, among others. 2. Responsible care and use of creation. 3. It prevents an interpretation from being presented as though it were directly stated by the text.</div></div>"],
["SSS 1","Islamic Studies","Qur’an, Revelation and Preservation","<div class=\"senior-textbook\"><h3>Learning objectives</h3><p>Explain the Islamic understanding of the Qur'an, describe the concept of revelation and outline the traditional account of preservation and compilation.</p><h3>The Qur'an</h3><p>In Islam, the Qur'an is regarded as the revealed word of Allah communicated to Prophet Muhammad through Angel Jibril. Revelation began in Makkah and continued over the prophetic mission.</p><h3>Preservation</h3><p>Muslim tradition emphasises both memorisation and written recording. After the Prophet's death, the early Muslim community undertook efforts to collect written materials and preserve a standard written text. Accounts of the compilation under Abu Bakr and the later standardisation associated with Caliph Uthman are important areas of study.</p><h3>Study discipline</h3><p>Distinguish the Qur'an itself from later explanatory works such as tafsir. When studying historical claims, identify whether a statement comes from Qur'anic text, hadith, classical scholarship or modern historical analysis.</p><h3>Exercises</h3><ol><li>What is revelation in Islamic terminology?</li><li>Name the angel traditionally associated with transmitting revelation.</li><li>Why is source distinction important?</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Divine communication revealed to prophets. 2. Angel Jibril (Gabriel). 3. Different sources have different roles and evidentiary status.</div></div>"],
["SSS 1","Nigerian History","Precolonial Nigerian Societies and Civilisations","<div class=\"senior-textbook\"><h3>Learning objectives</h3><p>Describe selected precolonial Nigerian societies, compare political and economic organisation and avoid treating Nigeria as a single uniform precolonial society.</p><h3>Historical diversity</h3><p>Before colonial rule, the territory now called Nigeria contained many societies with different political systems, languages, economies and belief systems. Examples include the Hausa states, Kanem-Borno, Oyo, Benin and numerous Igbo and other communities.</p><h3>Institutions</h3><table><tr><th>Area</th><th>Questions historians ask</th></tr><tr><td>Government</td><td>Who exercised authority? How was succession handled?</td></tr><tr><td>Economy</td><td>What was produced, exchanged or taxed?</td></tr><tr><td>Society</td><td>How were age, family, status and occupation organised?</td></tr><tr><td>Culture</td><td>Which languages, arts, religions and traditions shaped identity?</td></tr></table><h3>Historical method</h3><p>Use oral traditions, archaeology, material culture, written records and comparative scholarship critically. A source may preserve valuable information while also reflecting the perspective and limitations of its creator.</p><h3>Exercises</h3><ol><li>Why is it inaccurate to describe precolonial Nigeria as one political system?</li><li>Name three forms of historical evidence.</li><li>What is source criticism?</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. The region contained many distinct societies and institutions. 2. Oral traditions, archaeology and written records, among others. 3. Evaluating a source's origin, purpose, context, reliability and limitations.</div></div>"],
["SSS 1","French","Greetings, Introductions and Everyday Communication","<div class=\"senior-textbook\"><h3>Learning objectives</h3><p>Use basic French greetings, introduce oneself, ask simple questions and distinguish formal from informal forms where appropriate.</p><h3>Core expressions</h3><table><tr><th>French</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Bonjour</td><td>Hello / Good morning / Good day</td></tr><tr><td>Bonsoir</td><td>Good evening</td></tr><tr><td>Au revoir</td><td>Goodbye</td></tr><tr><td>Comment allez-vous ?</td><td>How are you? (formal/plural)</td></tr><tr><td>Comment vas-tu ?</td><td>How are you? (informal singular)</td></tr><tr><td>Je m'appelle...</td><td>My name is...</td></tr></table><h3>Mini-dialogue</h3><div class=\"box\"><strong>A:</strong> Bonjour. Comment allez-vous ?<br><strong>B:</strong> Bonjour. Je vais bien, merci. Et vous ?<br><strong>A:</strong> Je vais bien. Je m'appelle Ada. Et vous ?<br><strong>B:</strong> Je m'appelle Paul.</div><h3>Pronunciation note</h3><p>French pronunciation cannot be learned reliably from English spelling alone. Learners should listen to a teacher or reliable audio model and practise the sounds repeatedly.</p><h3>Exercises</h3><ol><li>Translate “Goodbye.”</li><li>Give a formal way to ask “How are you?”</li><li>Write a two-line self-introduction in French.</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Au revoir. 2. Comment allez-vous ? 3. Example: Je m'appelle Chidi. Je suis étudiant(e).</div></div>"],
["SSS 1","Arabic","Arabic Alphabet, Orthography and Pronunciation","<div class=\"senior-textbook\"><h3>Learning objectives</h3><p>Recognise the Arabic writing direction, identify letters and begin connecting letters correctly in simple written forms.</p><h3>Writing system</h3><p>Arabic is normally written from right to left. Letters can change their written shape depending on their position in a word. Short vowels are commonly represented by diacritical marks, although ordinary Arabic writing often omits them.</p><h3>Selected letters</h3><table><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Name</th><th>Approximate sound</th></tr><tr><td>ا</td><td>Alif</td><td>Long ā / carrier in common spelling patterns</td></tr><tr><td>ب</td><td>Bāʾ</td><td>b</td></tr><tr><td>ت</td><td>Tāʾ</td><td>t</td></tr><tr><td>م</td><td>Mīm</td><td>m</td></tr><tr><td>ن</td><td>Nūn</td><td>n</td></tr></table><h3>Practice</h3><p>Copy each letter slowly, then practise connecting letters where the script permits. Focus on correct direction, proportions and distinguishing letters by their dots.</p><h3>Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>Arabic letters are not simply isolated symbols; many change shape according to position.</li><li>Transliteration is only an approximation and cannot replace learning the Arabic script.</li></ul><h3>Exercises</h3><ol><li>Which direction is Arabic normally written?</li><li>Why do some letters have different forms in words?</li><li>What is the function of diacritics?</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Right to left. 2. Their joining and positional forms change. 3. They can indicate short vowels and other pronunciation information.</div></div>"],
["SSS 1","Visual Arts","Elements and Principles of Art and Design","<div class=\"senior-textbook\"><h3>Learning objectives</h3><p>Identify the basic elements of art and explain how principles of design organise them into effective compositions.</p><h3>Elements</h3><table><tr><th>Element</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Line</td><td>A mark with direction and length.</td></tr><tr><td>Shape</td><td>A two-dimensional enclosed area.</td></tr><tr><td>Form</td><td>Three-dimensional mass or the illusion of volume.</td></tr><tr><td>Colour</td><td>A visual property produced through light and pigment interactions.</td></tr><tr><td>Texture</td><td>Actual or implied surface quality.</td></tr><tr><td>Space</td><td>Area occupied or left between/around forms.</td></tr></table><h3>Principles</h3><p>Balance, contrast, emphasis, rhythm, unity, proportion and movement help organise elements. A design can deliberately create tension by using contrast or asymmetry rather than always seeking visual uniformity.</p><h3>Practical project</h3><p>Create a simple composition using at least five elements and three principles. Annotate where each is used and explain the intended visual effect.</p><h3>Exercises</h3><ol><li>Differentiate shape and form.</li><li>Name four principles of design.</li><li>How can contrast create emphasis?</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Shape is two-dimensional; form is three-dimensional or represents volume. 2. Balance, contrast, emphasis and rhythm, among others. 3. A strong difference in size, value, colour or other feature can draw attention to a focal area.</div></div>"],
["SSS 1","Music","Elements of Music: Rhythm, Melody and Harmony","<div class=\"senior-textbook\"><h3>Learning objectives</h3><p>Define basic musical elements, distinguish rhythm from melody and harmony and analyse a simple musical passage.</p><h3>Rhythm</h3><p>Rhythm concerns the organisation of durations and accents in time. A beat provides a regular pulse, while rhythm arranges longer and shorter sounds and rests around that pulse.</p><h3>Melody</h3><p>Melody is an organised succession of pitches perceived as a musical line. It has contour, range and rhythmic shape.</p><h3>Harmony</h3><p>Harmony concerns the sounding and relationship of pitches, particularly simultaneous pitches and the chords or progressions they create.</p><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Clap a steady pulse while a partner performs a contrasting rhythm. Then sing or play a short melody and identify its high and low points. If instruments are available, add a simple sustained supporting note and discuss the resulting harmonic effect.</p><h3>Misconceptions</h3><ul><li>Rhythm is not simply “the beat”; it is the pattern of durations and accents.</li><li>Melody and harmony are related but not interchangeable.</li></ul><h3>Exercises</h3><ol><li>Define rhythm.</li><li>What is melody?</li><li>What does harmony describe?</li></ol><div class=\"box\"><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Organisation of durations and accents in time. 2. An organised succession of pitches forming a musical line. 3. Relationships among pitches, especially simultaneous pitches and chords.</div></div>"]
];
